# pi-gen config for the Gexis Player image.
#
# Only stage0/1/2 (Raspberry Pi OS Lite) plus our own stage-gexis are built —
# stage3/4/5 are the Desktop stages and are deliberately not in STAGE_LIST.
# stage-gexis is bind-mounted into the container by the root Makefile
# (PIGEN_DOCKER_OPTS), so it lives outside the pinned pi-gen submodule.
#
# This file is sourced twice: once by build-docker.sh on the host (under
# set -eu, before pi-gen's own BASE_DIR exists) and once by build.sh inside
# the container. /pi-gen is hardcoded rather than using ${BASE_DIR} so the
# host-side sourcing doesn't fail on an unbound variable — we only ever
# build via the Docker wrapper, where pi-gen always lives at /pi-gen
# (Dockerfile: COPY . /pi-gen/).

IMG_NAME="gexis-player"

STAGE_LIST="/pi-gen/stage0 /pi-gen/stage1 /pi-gen/stage2 /pi-gen/stage-gexis"

# ADR-0021 chose the systemd/firstrun.sh first-boot mechanism, not
# cloud-init (stage-gexis/01-firstboot installs our own firstrun.sh).
# Cloud-init's own stage2/04-cloud-init otherwise ships inert, unedited
# Ubuntu cloud-config templates on the boot partition regardless of whether
# anything on the image reads them — misleading, not just unused.
ENABLE_CLOUD_INIT=0

# Deploy the raw .img rather than pi-gen's default .zip, 2026-09-13.
#
# The deciding reason is George's, and it outranks the rest: Claude builds,
# George flashes, and a raw .img goes straight into Raspberry Pi Imager
# with nothing in between. The build host's convenience does not get to
# add a step to the one part of this loop a human actually performs.
#
# Supporting, not deciding: bmaptool (which pi-gen already emits a .bmap
# for, see export-image/05-finalise) can skip unallocated blocks only when
# handed the image rather than an archive, and mtools can read the boot
# partition straight out of it - see image/README.md.
#
# Measured cost, since it is not free: the zip is 1.28GB against a 4.5GB
# raw image, so this trades ~3.2GB of disk and of `docker cp` streaming
# (build-docker.sh:154 copies the whole deploy dir out on every run)
# against the CPU time of zip -6. `make prune` is what keeps the disk side
# bounded; `make fetch-deploy` is what recovers the streaming side when it
# is killed. Compressing again to make Claude's copy-out cheaper would be
# optimising the wrong end of the loop.
#
# Note this changes the deployed filename: with compression, pi-gen names
# the archive "${ARCHIVE_FILENAME}${IMG_SUFFIX}" ("image_2026-…zip"); with
# none it copies "${IMG_FILENAME}${IMG_SUFFIX}.img" ("2026-…img"), no
# "image_" prefix. The Makefile's manifest glob (*-gexis-player*.info) is
# unaffected - it matches the .info, which is copied either way.
DEPLOY_COMPRESSION=none
